      "card_ids": ["C01","C07","C10","C11","C18","C19","C26","C28"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4/L5 permeability records show steroid-like molecules are highly permeable: megestrol acetate is BCS class II/highly permeable, testosterone Caco-2 Papp values are 5.1–12.0 ×10^-5 cm/s and 33 ×10^-6 cm/s, exemestane Caco-2 Papp is 4.71 ×10^-5 cm/s, prednisolone human Peff is 4.4 ×10^-4 cm/s, hydrocortisone has high passive intestinal permeability, and budesonide shows high bidirectional permeability with no efflux.",
      "card_ids": ["C47","C44","C50","C51","C48","C52","C49","C45","C54","C55","C56","C57","C59","C60","C63"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic clearance/metabolic stability records show steroid analogs (testosterone, epitestosterone, 17-OHPC, MPA) undergo hepatic metabolism via CYP3A4/5 and UGT1A9 with moderate-to-high intrinsic clearance, providing a mechanistic concern for first-pass loss; however, these are analog in vitro/clearance predictions, not query-specific F outcomes, and do not affirmatively establish F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C65","C66","C67","C68","C69","C72","C74"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No supplied record provides a direct or transferable low absolute oral bioavailability outcome for the query; rolipram low F in rabbit (3–4%) and rhesus monkey (<0.1%) are species-specific and not applicable to this steroid, and BCRP efflux of estrone-3-sulfate is for an anionic conjugate, not the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C15","C24","C61"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Poor aqueous solubility of steroid analogs (megestrol acetate 2 µg/mL, chlormadinone acetate 0.4 µg/mL) is a formulation concern, but BCS II/high permeability and formulation can still yield F ≥ 20%; solubility limitation alone is not affirmative evidence for F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C43","C42"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "contradictory",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C65","C66","C67","C68","C69","C72","C74"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule in the supplied records.",
    "No matched oral versus IV exposure data for the query; first-pass extraction and ester hydrolysis are not quantified.",
    "L6 hepatic clearance/metabolic stability records are for steroid analogs (testosterone, epitestosterone, 17-OHPC, MPA), not the query, with species and assay-system differences.",
    "No query-specific formulation or dissolution data; high logD (5.54) and predicted poor solubility remain formulation concerns.",
    "Transfer from steroid contexts is based on structural class rather than exact formulation, dose, and condition matching."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains high: transferable steroid contexts report F at or above 20% (EE 25–65%, 17-methyltestosterone good/relative ~90–100%, estetrol 80% human/33% mouse, projected steroid-like compound 40–70%) and L4/L5 permeability records show steroid-like molecules are highly permeable; L6 hepatic metabolism records for steroid analogs raise a first-pass concern but are indirect and do not affirmatively establish F < 20%. Confidence is moderate because no direct query F or first-pass quantification is available."
}

